{
  "title": "Sagrada Familia",
  "indexDescription": "Sagrada Familia in 2009",
  "description": "We visited the Sagrada Família in late 2009 during a three-week family trip to Spain over Christmas.\n\nWe arrived in the morning and joined a line that stretched around the building. After waiting about an hour and a half, we finally made it inside. Construction and scaffolding were everywhere. After exploring for a few minutes, we discovered another line inside. We had no idea what it was for, but decided to join it anyway. After another 45-minute wait, we learned that it was for a free elevator ride to the top!\n\nAs soon as we stepped out onto a narrow catwalk high above the ground, the bells began to ring. It was exactly noon. The sound was deafening, especially since we were only a few feet away. What sounded like bells were actually large speakers, close enough to touch. Between the tremendous noise and the dizzying height, Kathleen and Elisabeth quickly made their way back down to the ground. Meanwhile, Alex, Tom, and I waited for the ringing to stop, took our hands away from our ears, and leisurely explored the upper levels. We stopped at numerous window openings to enjoy spectacular views of Barcelona and get close-up looks at the basilica’s remarkable organic-looking façades.\n\nConstruction of the Sagrada Família began in 1882, based on the design of Catalan architect Antoni Gaudí. When we visited in 2009, the central towers were missing; my photo shows their foundations capped off high above the building. In 2025, the installation of the central tower’s upper sections made the Sagrada Família the tallest church in the world. Its extraordinary century-spanning construction is largely financed by visitor admission fees.",
